@@ -69,6 +69,29 @@ The :meth:`~rich.console.Console.log` methods offers the same capabilities as pr
 
 To help with debugging, the log() method has a ``log_locals`` parameter. If you set this to ``True``, Rich will display a table of local variables where the method was called.
 
+Printing JSON
+-------------
+
+Use :meth:`~rich.console.Console.print_json` to pretty print a JSON document supplied as a string. The output is re-serialized as multi-line JSON, indented by 4 spaces by default (set ``indent`` to change this), and syntax highlighted unless you pass ``highlight=False``::
+
+    >>> console.print_json('[false, true, null, "foo"]')
+
+The same output is available from the package root via ``rich.print_json``, which uses the global console::
+
+    >>> from rich import print_json
+    >>> print_json('[false, true, null, "foo"]')
+
+Internally the JSON is wrapped in a :class:`~rich.json.JSON` renderable, so you can also pass it to :meth:`~rich.console.Console.print` or :meth:`~rich.console.Console.log`::
+
+    >>> from rich.json import JSON
+    >>> console.log(JSON('{"name": "Rich"}'))
+
+The renderable turns off word wrapping for its lines. Under default printing, lines wider than the console are still cropped to the console width. Pass ``soft_wrap=True`` to :meth:`~rich.console.Console.print` to keep each line whole.
+
+The ``rich.json`` module can also be run from the command line to pretty print a JSON file::
+
+    python -m rich.json path/to/file.json
+
